1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,826
BatchScope is a desktop prototype for reviewing pharmaceutical quality
operations. This walkthrough uses fictional data to connect summary

2
00:00:06,826 --> 00:00:09,018
metrics with the records behind them.

3
00:00:10,250 --> 00:00:17,855
The Windows release runs without installing Python. Here, Generate
creates a reproducible dataset of one hundred batches, with linked

4
00:00:17,855 --> 00:00:21,857
measurements, deviations and actions. All processing stays on the
computer.

5
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:29,386
For June thirtieth, the snapshot contains one hundred and ninety-eight
measurements. Fourteen are missing, leaving one hundred and

6
00:00:29,386 --> 00:00:34,322
eighty-four judgeable results. Missing values remain missing; they are
never converted to zero.

7
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:42,431
Open a measurement group and filter the underlying records. Values,
methods, units and example bounds support review. Seventy-four

8
00:00:42,431 --> 00:00:48,547
measurements fall outside fictional ranges; that count is not an
industry benchmark or an investigation conclusion.

9
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:56,820
The quality review workbench separates overdue actions, actions due
today, and actions due within seven days. Its default view focuses on

10
00:00:56,820 --> 00:01:02,262
these items. Time urgency helps organize follow-up; it does not measure
patient or product risk.

11
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:10,592
A separate filter identifies outstanding actions linked to closed
deviations. Eleven appear in this snapshot. These require context and

12
00:01:10,592 --> 00:01:14,872
follow-up; the software does not automatically label them as procedural
violations.

13
00:01:15,550 --> 00:01:23,355
Select an action to open its batch overview. The tabs connect
measurements, deviations and all actions visible at the same date,
including

14
00:01:23,355 --> 00:01:27,612
completed actions. This makes record tracing possible without losing the
snapshot context.

15
00:01:28,550 --> 00:01:35,851
Backlog age groups show where open deviations accumulate by product and
category. The example age bands are mutually exclusive. Their

16
00:01:35,851 --> 00:01:40,597
counts reconcile with the twenty-two open deviations, without imposing a
regulatory time limit.

17
00:01:41,250 --> 00:01:48,338
Monthly movements separate opening backlog, new openings, closures and
ending backlog. In June, twenty plus five minus three equals

18
00:01:48,338 --> 00:01:54,307
twenty-two. The final period ends at the analysis date, so a partial
month stays clearly bounded.

19
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:02,151
Now compare June thirtieth with July thirty-first using the same input
snapshot. Open deviations decrease from twenty-two to seventeen,

20
00:02:02,151 --> 00:02:07,797
while overdue actions rise from thirty to thirty-three. The comparison
exposes changes behind these totals.

21
00:02:08,450 --> 00:02:16,266
Four actions enter the ending overdue list, while one previously overdue
action completes. The net increase is three. Searches and record

22
00:02:16,266 --> 00:02:21,477
identifiers support follow-up, while the software avoids claiming that
these movements establish root causes.

23
00:02:22,150 --> 00:02:30,021
The offline bilingual report includes a chart, review tables and input
fingerprints. CSV exports and SQLite support further analysis.

24
00:02:30,021 --> 00:02:35,407
Hashes identify file contents; they are not electronic signatures or
proof of compliance.

25
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:43,540
BatchScope demonstrates data modelling, SQL analysis and clear quality-
review logic. It is an open-source portfolio prototype, not a

26
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:49,327
validated production system. The next step is independent feedback from
teachers or quality professionals.

